WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en-GB

00:00:05.060 --> 00:00:09.700
Apologies from Stephen Hay, from Wol Kolade
and from Victor Adebowale, both of whom had

00:00:09.700 --> 00:00:14.799
to leave at lunchtime. Has anyone got any
declarations of interest that needed to be

00:00:14.799 --> 00:00:22.539
added to our registers? No? Good.
Can we just start by approving the draft minutes

00:00:22.539 --> 00:00:28.800
of meeting – the meetings held in common
on the 24th of May? Any comments or corrections

00:00:28.800 --> 00:00:35.160
to those minutes? Excellent; we will take
those as approved.

00:00:35.160 --> 00:00:40.450
So, if we can move to the first item of the
agenda – where’s Pauline? What we’ll

00:00:40.450 --> 00:00:46.100
do, if Pauline, then Matthew, and then is
it Elizabeth rather than Paul? Just give us

00:00:46.100 --> 00:00:51.410
an overview of the winter operational and
then the finance issues, and then we’ll

00:00:51.410 --> 00:00:55.720
open it up for discussion. Pauline?
Okay, so I’m going to start by talking to

00:00:55.720 --> 00:01:03.700
the paper on winter, and I think most people
will have had an opportunity to read the paper

00:01:03.700 --> 00:01:08.060
and will be pretty up to date on the various
issues within it. But just to highlight a

00:01:08.060 --> 00:01:15.770
few. I think first of all, reflecting on last
winter, in an earlier meeting, we discussed

00:01:15.770 --> 00:01:20.880
the winter review, and that has since been
published. So, clearly, we have taken the

00:01:20.880 --> 00:01:28.640
learning from last winter and incorporated
it into our planning for the months ahead.

00:01:28.640 --> 00:01:33.549
I would like to take this opportunity to remind
people that despite the challenges that have

00:01:33.549 --> 00:01:41.810
taken place last winter, the NHS actually
managed to see 68,000 more people within four

00:01:41.810 --> 00:01:47.299
hours during that peak winter period, and
I think the reason why I’m making that point

00:01:47.299 --> 00:01:53.310
is that I think it’s really important that
this meeting, and indeed, any similar meetings,

00:01:53.310 --> 00:01:59.219
takes an opportunity to thank our staff for
the tremendous work they did, and that they

00:01:59.219 --> 00:02:06.810
see some headlines on those issues as well
as on the challenges that they faced.

00:02:06.810 --> 00:02:13.300
Just to bring people up to date and to say
that an enormous amount of work has taken

00:02:13.300 --> 00:02:22.189
place across the NHS since last winter with
a significant focus on transformation. All

00:02:22.189 --> 00:02:27.430
members of the Board will be very much aware
of the vision for the future as far as the

00:02:27.430 --> 00:02:33.840
delivery of urgent and emergency care is concerned,
and having a very different model to the model

00:02:33.840 --> 00:02:41.709
that we’ve sort of all grown up with. And
in the last six months and more, there’s

00:02:41.709 --> 00:02:48.980
been a real focus on the component parts that
we need to get in place to deliver that vision.

00:02:48.980 --> 00:02:53.930
So, just to highlight a few; they are set
out in the paper. But, for example, if we

00:02:53.930 --> 00:03:01.069
look at our 111 service, the number of calls
being handled each month have grown significantly,

00:03:01.069 --> 00:03:10.920
by about 10%. Really importantly, over 51%
of those calls are now being dealt with by

00:03:10.920 --> 00:03:17.459
a clinician. If you remember, we had an ambition
for 50% – 100% coverage but 50% of our calls

00:03:17.459 --> 00:03:24.800
– and we have managed to exceed that.
If we move to what happens within hospitals,

00:03:24.800 --> 00:03:32.959
we’ve set up GP streaming in all emergency
departments across the NHS where we feel patients

00:03:32.959 --> 00:03:38.190
can benefit by having GP streaming, where
patients with minor illness are arriving.

00:03:38.190 --> 00:03:45.750
And we’ve also made real progress in developing
emergency day care, an increase of about 10%

00:03:45.750 --> 00:03:52.569
year on year. Within the hospitals, we’ve
had a significant focus on length of stay,

00:03:52.569 --> 00:03:58.520
which is very important to us moving forward,
and not only have we managed to reduce the

00:03:58.520 --> 00:04:06.799
number of beds on the back of our DTOC work
by about 2,200, which is equivalent to a number

00:04:06.799 --> 00:04:12.640
of acute hospitals, depending on the size
of them, but also, on our length of stay work,

00:04:12.640 --> 00:04:19.709
for patients who’ve been in hospital for
longer than 21 days, we set ourselves an ambition

00:04:19.709 --> 00:04:26.970
around 4,000, to reduce it by 4,000, and so
far, we have reduced by the equivalent of

00:04:26.970 --> 00:04:31.740
1,697 beds.
Outside hospital, we’ve had a significant

00:04:31.740 --> 00:04:38.560
focus on preparing for winter as far as GP
access is concerned, extended access, and

00:04:38.560 --> 00:04:47.240
that will be in place by October. Our ambulance
service is working through its own transformation

00:04:47.240 --> 00:04:52.979
programme. The new standards have been implemented
across all ambulance services, and month on

00:04:52.979 --> 00:04:58.300
month, we can see an ongoing improvement in
each of those standards.

00:04:58.300 --> 00:05:04.740
As far as workforce is concerned, we’ve
had a particular focus on our ED workforce,

00:05:04.740 --> 00:05:10.180
an increase in consultant numbers, an increase
in physician assistance, and an increase in

00:05:10.180 --> 00:05:16.090
trainee doctors.
As well as those areas of transformation,

00:05:16.090 --> 00:05:24.030
we’ve taken a number of specific actions
in order to prepare for the winter period.

00:05:24.030 --> 00:05:30.199
The first one to highlight, again, set out
in the report, is the work on flu. I don’t

00:05:30.199 --> 00:05:35.190
think I need to remind anybody around the
table about the challenges that we faced last

00:05:35.190 --> 00:05:41.860
winter. We made a very early decision this
year about the vaccine that we would use for

00:05:41.860 --> 00:05:50.720
– across our general population, for elderly
people and for our younger citizens, using

00:05:50.720 --> 00:05:55.880
quadrivalent for our younger citizens, and
the adjuvanted trivalent vaccine for people

00:05:55.880 --> 00:06:01.650
over 65. And again, we’ve set out in the
paper the impact we believe that would have,

00:06:01.650 --> 00:06:09.610
as well as increasing the distribution of
vaccines to children. We’ve spent a period

00:06:09.610 --> 00:06:16.330
of time working with professional organisation,
trade unions and our staff, to talk about

00:06:16.330 --> 00:06:26.199
how we can deliver an ambition of 100% vaccination
cover for our staff within the NHS, and we

00:06:26.199 --> 00:06:30.500
are launching a programme about that as we
speak.

00:06:30.500 --> 00:06:38.940
Other specific actions to support winter:
we’ve put £145 million into our hospitals

00:06:38.940 --> 00:06:47.009
and some of our community services, to increase
bed capacity, to increase capacity to support

00:06:47.009 --> 00:06:54.919
emergency day care, etc. We’ve had a significant
focus on our elective programme, to monitor

00:06:54.919 --> 00:07:02.229
very carefully what is happening with patients
who are long-waits, 52-week patients – 52-week-plus

00:07:02.229 --> 00:07:09.139
patients, but also, on the full programme,
so as to avoid a situation occurring where

00:07:09.139 --> 00:07:14.970
we have to have short-term cancellations or
short-notice cancellations this winter. So,

00:07:14.970 --> 00:07:21.860
a lot of work taking place there.
We’ve also had a significant focus on our

00:07:21.860 --> 00:07:29.100
poorly performing organisations, identified
who they are, and we’ve been working intensively

00:07:29.100 --> 00:07:38.210
with them over the recent months. And in addition
to all of that, we are putting back in place

00:07:38.210 --> 00:07:45.360
the same arrangements that we had last year
from a national/regional local focus, to support

00:07:45.360 --> 00:07:49.580
our frontline staff.
So, I think that gives a very quick summary

00:07:49.580 --> 00:07:56.129
of the work that has taken place on the transformation
front and on the specific actions that we

00:07:56.129 --> 00:08:03.450
are taking to prepare for winter. Clearly,
we recognise that the coming months will be

00:08:03.450 --> 00:08:11.800
a challenge. We are concerned about the level
of occupancy within our hospitals, and we’re

00:08:11.800 --> 00:08:17.960
particularly concerned about the ask of our
staff. We know that they’ve had a very difficult

00:08:17.960 --> 00:08:25.379
year during the summer months. The heatwave
caused us problems as well as the cold weather

00:08:25.379 --> 00:08:31.789
in the winter, and, you know, we know that
people have had to work long hours, etc.,

00:08:31.789 --> 00:08:35.220
to support the care that we want to provide
for our patients.

00:08:35.220 --> 00:08:40.159
And I think that’s enough from me for now.
Thank you, Pauline. I think we would all want

00:08:40.159 --> 00:08:46.260
to put on record how grateful we are for the
work that people across the country in the

00:08:46.260 --> 00:08:50.810
Service are doing in what we recognise is
a very pressured time, that there hasn’t

00:08:50.810 --> 00:08:55.580
been much of a let-up through the summer at
all. And, you know, we know how hard people

00:08:55.580 --> 00:09:00.180
are working to deliver the standards of care
that they want to deliver and that people

00:09:00.180 --> 00:09:04.399
expect. We also recognise how hard you and
your team are working, both in NHS England

00:09:04.399 --> 00:09:07.430
and in NHS Improvement, to support them, so
thank you.

00:09:07.430 --> 00:09:10.800
Matthew, do you want to take us to the operational
performance?

00:09:10.800 --> 00:09:19.320
Yes, let me just pick up the other areas beyond
emergency care, for you. The numbers are all

00:09:19.320 --> 00:09:24.720
in the paper, so I won’t walk you through
those. Let me just talk a bit about sort of

00:09:24.720 --> 00:09:29.880
the headlines. So, around waiting lists, we
set ourselves a target of maintaining waiting

00:09:29.880 --> 00:09:35.540
list at its current level and reducing long-waiters.
We have seen an increase in the waiting list

00:09:35.540 --> 00:09:42.010
so far this year, and an increase in long-waiters.
So, through the regions, we’re working with

00:09:42.010 --> 00:09:47.600
every hospital to put in place the processes
to ensure that the number of patients waiting

00:09:47.600 --> 00:09:53.070
more than 52 weeks are reduced back, to halve
it, with an ambition to reduce it back to

00:09:53.070 --> 00:09:58.730
zero. But – so putting in those sort of
rigorous processes is going on at the moment.

00:09:58.730 --> 00:10:04.560
And we’re working through with hospitals
how we increase the volumes of activity that

00:10:04.560 --> 00:10:08.240
are being done, recognising the pressures
that are on beds that Pauline talked about,

00:10:08.240 --> 00:10:12.920
but the need to make sure that patients aren’t
waiting unnecessarily for outpatients and

00:10:12.920 --> 00:10:18.070
aren’t waiting unnecessarily for day surgery,
which are not curtailed by access to inpatient

00:10:18.070 --> 00:10:23.660
capacity in order to manage the waiting lists
back done.

00:10:23.660 --> 00:10:31.520
In cancer, we got within touching distance
of the 85% target at the end of last financial

00:10:31.520 --> 00:10:39.080
year, so it was with considerable frustration
that we saw that our compliance was torn to

00:10:39.080 --> 00:10:48.310
78% on our 62-day-wait cancer target. That
is being driven by a very large spike in demand,

00:10:48.310 --> 00:10:52.050
through increased public consciousness, particularly
around prostate/urology cancer. In February,

00:10:52.050 --> 00:10:59.170
the – there was much news about that had
become the major cancer killer above breast

00:10:59.170 --> 00:11:05.480
cancer, and that’s led to a – something
like a 14% increase in the total number of

00:11:05.480 --> 00:11:11.470
cancer referrals, something like a 26% increase
in the number of urology referrals. So – which

00:11:11.470 --> 00:11:16.410
has caused this extension in waiting times.
Now, we would continue to encourage the public

00:11:16.410 --> 00:11:20.450
to come forward if they suspect that they
may have the symptoms of cancer, and it is

00:11:20.450 --> 00:11:24.820
very important that we screen and identify
early, and therefore, the challenge for the

00:11:24.820 --> 00:11:32.839
NHS is to make sure that we can respond to
that growth in demand appropriately. So, we

00:11:32.839 --> 00:11:38.329
currently have distributed – in the process
of distributing £10 million in order to – across

00:11:38.329 --> 00:11:42.230
the NHS to deal with the backlog that was
created by that – with that increase in

00:11:42.230 --> 00:11:46.399
activity, whilst we continue to make sure
that best practice in focusing on every patient

00:11:46.399 --> 00:11:50.780
on a cancer pathway is happening on every
one – in every one of our institutions,

00:11:50.780 --> 00:11:55.769
and we’re working to make sure that the
best-practice pathways are put in place across

00:11:55.769 --> 00:12:02.160
the system.
And the last thing I would just touch on is

00:12:02.160 --> 00:12:06.709
on our mental health programme, where, in
terms of our ambitions around the Five Year

00:12:06.709 --> 00:12:12.540
Forward View, we are making good progress
and are still expecting to hit the objectives.

00:12:12.540 --> 00:12:17.570
But we are also looking now at making sure
that work around mental health is also supporting

00:12:17.570 --> 00:12:22.010
in the run-up to winter – supporting the
whole of the system to ensure that patients

00:12:22.010 --> 00:12:25.639
who have mental-health problems don’t find
themselves lengthy times in A&amp;Es when they

00:12:25.639 --> 00:12:29.149
should be more appropriately being cared for
somewhere else. So, there’s both the, where

00:12:29.149 --> 00:12:31.860
are we going to be in the next two or three
years, and where do we need to be in the next

00:12:31.860 --> 00:12:35.839
two or three months in order to make sure
that winter flows well? Thank you.

00:12:35.839 --> 00:12:40.440
Thank you. Elizabeth?
Yeah, so before we go on to the financial

00:12:40.440 --> 00:12:44.630
consequences of the performance discussion
we’ve just had, I think it does – I think

00:12:44.630 --> 00:12:48.750
it warrants just for noting that this is the
first time – and it’s a pleasure, actually

00:12:48.750 --> 00:12:53.940
– but this is the first time that NHSI and
E have actually prepared a joint finance report

00:12:53.940 --> 00:12:59.860
that looks holistically across finance, across
both commissioning and provision. That’s

00:12:59.860 --> 00:13:04.850
not been without its challenges, mainly because
there’s some technical different ways in

00:13:04.850 --> 00:13:10.330
which we report, and we can always, you know,
pick some of those questions up offline, or

00:13:10.330 --> 00:13:15.730
I can ask Paul to explain. But what I would
like to do is just give you a brief update

00:13:15.730 --> 00:13:21.170
about where we are in terms of the planning
process, and – because clearly, at our last

00:13:21.170 --> 00:13:26.180
joint board meeting, we committed to working
with the sector to ensure that we delivered

00:13:26.180 --> 00:13:31.279
a balanced plan. So, the last time we met,
we were talking about having a three-stage

00:13:31.279 --> 00:13:37.870
process to improve the planning position for
the sector in aggregate. A lot of work has

00:13:37.870 --> 00:13:43.210
happened, and good progress has been made.
But throughout that process and as that work

00:13:43.210 --> 00:13:48.830
has happened, both at the system level and
bespoke discussions with individual trusts

00:13:48.830 --> 00:13:55.459
in particular that were the most challenged,
we have also identified another – a number

00:13:55.459 --> 00:13:59.430
of other risks. So, some of those will be
expressed around some of the challenges coming

00:13:59.430 --> 00:14:03.740
back about the agenda for change discussions
that we’re currently having with the Service

00:14:03.740 --> 00:14:10.630
and making sure that those – that commitment
is fully funded. And equally, you know, we

00:14:10.630 --> 00:14:15.269
did not expect to have had at this stage of
the year some of the occupancy issues that

00:14:15.269 --> 00:14:18.850
have been more eloquently described by Pauline
and Matthew.

00:14:18.850 --> 00:14:24.300
So, where we stand at the moment is, we’ve
closed more than at least half of the gap.

00:14:24.300 --> 00:14:29.810
The work continues, and there is an absolute
strong commitment across I and E and the Service,

00:14:29.810 --> 00:14:37.579
actually, that we need to close this gap before
quarter two and go on to deliver this balanced

00:14:37.579 --> 00:14:43.690
plan. Because most importantly, this is the
platform for the long-term plan, and if we

00:14:43.690 --> 00:14:48.029
end up in a position this year where we are
– we have any financial risk that needs

00:14:48.029 --> 00:14:53.170
to be carried forward into future years, obviously,
that will affect our ability to really drive

00:14:53.170 --> 00:14:57.980
change, and that really is important, and
something that we’re really getting back

00:14:57.980 --> 00:15:04.579
in spades from the workstream leads.
So, as at month four, table one within the

00:15:04.579 --> 00:15:10.889
report, you know, summarises the position.
So, we’re largely on plan, or broadly on

00:15:10.889 --> 00:15:18.240
plan. There’s further work to do in terms
of closing the planning gap, but I think we

00:15:18.240 --> 00:15:23.639
can say that it’s a positive place to be
in at this stage of the year, in respect of

00:15:23.639 --> 00:15:28.130
the fact that the Board-approved plans that
have come forward from all of our providers

00:15:28.130 --> 00:15:32.880
and the commissioning sector are in aggregate
being delivered, so I think that’s a really

00:15:32.880 --> 00:15:37.850
positive thing to say.
It is of course not without its risks, and

00:15:37.850 --> 00:15:43.779
we also highlight within the report, there
is a level of risk which, whilst we have much

00:15:43.779 --> 00:15:50.050
greater clarity on the risk and a better understanding,
and it’s more triangulated, we no longer

00:15:50.050 --> 00:15:54.579
have a risk reserve to offset that risk, so
between now and the end of the year, we are

00:15:54.579 --> 00:16:00.230
going to be looking at a number of options,
and us working with systems, really, to say,

00:16:00.230 --> 00:16:04.839
you know, ‘What are the trade-offs to ensure
that we don’t forfeit some of the future

00:16:04.839 --> 00:16:16.380
by making bad decisions this year?’
Thank you very much. Can I open up the discussion?

00:16:16.380 --> 00:16:24.930
Looking down a long line of people. No additional
comments? Everyone happy to take those as

00:16:24.930 --> 00:16:27.610
read? Ian? Simon? Anything from either of
you?

00:16:27.610 --> 00:16:34.139
Yeah, I mean, I wonder if it’s worth saying
a little bit about the importance of the flu

00:16:34.139 --> 00:16:36.310
vaccination programme for –
I think that’s a good idea.

00:16:36.310 --> 00:16:41.220
As part of our winter preparedness. I mean,
I think, you know, the general story is, as

00:16:41.220 --> 00:16:47.670
Pauline outlined, that the – you know, that
this has been a tough-demand year, and occupancy

00:16:47.670 --> 00:16:52.850
is high, but against that, there’s been
an awful lot of transformation work, and the

00:16:52.850 --> 00:16:58.940
NHS is putting a lot of cash out there to
help those places that need it with new capacity.

00:16:58.940 --> 00:17:04.220
So, there’s a lot of good planning going
on. I think the NHS faces the winter with

00:17:04.220 --> 00:17:10.930
an awful lot of effort at local, national
levels, but as part of that, ensuring that

00:17:10.930 --> 00:17:16.340
our staff are protected across the winter
and able to support patients through vaccination

00:17:16.340 --> 00:17:20.570
is really important. So, at the risk of putting
one of my fellow directors on the spot, I

00:17:20.570 --> 00:17:22.250
don’t know if – Steve, whether there’s
a –

00:17:22.250 --> 00:17:24.750
Yeah –
I mean, we’ve clearly had a – you know,

00:17:24.750 --> 00:17:27.540
seen the importance of this in our winter
plans, and I wonder if you wanted to say anything?

00:17:27.540 --> 00:17:31.920
Yeah, so there’s two components of flu vaccinations.
One is the vaccination programmes that we

00:17:31.920 --> 00:17:37.420
run with Public Health England for the general
public, and the other is the vaccination programmes

00:17:37.420 --> 00:17:44.760
within staff in those institutions that deliver
healthcare. So, on the former, I think one

00:17:44.760 --> 00:17:50.200
of the things to say for the coming winter
is that we have a new vaccine, so we have

00:17:50.200 --> 00:17:56.380
a better vaccine for the over 65s, and the
Joint Committee on Vaccination recommended

00:17:56.380 --> 00:18:05.210
its usage with guidance subsequently from
PHE. And we alerted our general practitioners

00:18:05.210 --> 00:18:09.010
and commissioners to that earlier in the year,
and we have been working on the distribution

00:18:09.010 --> 00:18:14.470
of that new vaccine, which is always – because
it’s a new vaccine we haven’t used before,

00:18:14.470 --> 00:18:18.400
always requires a fair amount of planning,
so that is being distributed out, and that

00:18:18.400 --> 00:18:23.270
will give a better level of protection to
those particularly who are most vulnerable.

00:18:23.270 --> 00:18:31.630
So, we are very keen that particularly our
over 65s have access to that vaccine.

00:18:31.630 --> 00:18:38.430
On the staff side, we all recognise as professionals
– in fact, it is in professional guidance,

00:18:38.430 --> 00:18:44.510
that we have a duty to protect patients through
avoidance of things such as transmissible

00:18:44.510 --> 00:18:49.240
common illnesses, and we’ve worked really
closely with our professional colleagues this

00:18:49.240 --> 00:18:56.040
year – that’s colleagues such as Kathy,
Ruth and Jane – within NHSE, but also our

00:18:56.040 --> 00:19:01.110
chief professional officers, which cover many
other professions – allied healthcare professionals,

00:19:01.110 --> 00:19:07.270
pharmacists, etc. – to – and with our
colleagues outside NHS England and NHS Improvement

00:19:07.270 --> 00:19:11.500
– our Royal Colleges, the Royal College
of Nursing, and many of our union partners

00:19:11.500 --> 00:19:18.050
– to ensure that we deliver a consistent
message to our staff, that it is really important

00:19:18.050 --> 00:19:24.060
that staff are vaccinated, that vaccines are
effective, and they are the best way of protecting.

00:19:24.060 --> 00:19:29.350
And when you don’t initially have symptoms,
you are still in a position where you may

00:19:29.350 --> 00:19:33.801
be able to transmit the disease to colleagues
and patients, and we’ve put a particular

00:19:33.801 --> 00:19:39.710
focus on those areas where our most vulnerable
patients are treated, so those would be patients

00:19:39.710 --> 00:19:44.790
whose immune systems, either through disease
or through treatment, is compromised, and

00:19:44.790 --> 00:19:50.180
may be particularly susceptible to flu. And
we’ve asked our Medical Director and Nursing

00:19:50.180 --> 00:19:58.280
Director colleagues in our providers to ensure
that staff in those areas are vaccinated,

00:19:58.280 --> 00:20:04.660
and if they’re not, to look at the possibility
of whether redeployment is required, obviously

00:20:04.660 --> 00:20:10.100
balancing everything that one needs to balance
in terms of the safe running of those units.

00:20:10.100 --> 00:20:18.410
So, I would like to emphasise, as I’m sure
my colleagues around the table would, the

00:20:18.410 --> 00:20:25.070
critical importance of vaccination amongst
staff this winter, and we are really focused

00:20:25.070 --> 00:20:30.220
– and last year, we did better than the
year before, but we know there are some institutions

00:20:30.220 --> 00:20:33.730
that could do even better, and we have a real
focus on that this winter.

00:20:33.730 --> 00:20:43.870
Jane, you wanted to come in?
Yeah, just to build on what Steve said. So,

00:20:43.870 --> 00:20:45.900
yes, last year we had 68.7% of staff that
were vaccinated, but I think the really critical

00:20:45.900 --> 00:20:48.800
fact that we’ve done a lot more detailed
work on this over the recent months is that

00:20:48.800 --> 00:20:56.160
30%, over 30% of people can have flu, carry
the virus without actually knowing they’ve

00:20:56.160 --> 00:21:01.161
got it, and if you put – if that’s a member
of staff, whatever job they do, who are in

00:21:01.161 --> 00:21:05.840
contact with people, whether that’s in a
general practice surgery, whether it’s in

00:21:05.840 --> 00:21:10.940
a hospital, outpatients, whether it’s in
a ward, that really does put people at risk,

00:21:10.940 --> 00:21:16.010
and so it’s absolutely vital that staff
do take up the vaccine. It is effective, and

00:21:16.010 --> 00:21:20.490
we are very clear that we need to make that
as easy as possible for staff, so they should

00:21:20.490 --> 00:21:24.641
– it should be easy for them to be able
to have that vaccine, and I think we know

00:21:24.641 --> 00:21:29.680
that a lot of the organisations who delivered
very high percentages last year did that;

00:21:29.680 --> 00:21:33.580
they made it very easy for people to have
the vaccination, they were available, you

00:21:33.580 --> 00:21:37.280
know, throughout the different times of the
day and night, and I think that’s another

00:21:37.280 --> 00:21:38.870
critical part of this.
Matthew and Moira.

00:21:38.870 --> 00:21:43.960
Just one further point, knowing that many
millions of members of the public are listening

00:21:43.960 --> 00:21:47.510
to the live stream, it would be remiss not
to mention that for the second year running,

00:21:47.510 --> 00:21:53.550
the NHS is extending the free vaccine to social
workers, people working in care homes, people

00:21:53.550 --> 00:21:58.580
working in nursing homes. Don’t just protect
yourself, but protect the people that you’re

00:21:58.580 --> 00:22:04.530
caring for, and if you bring a proof of identity
of where you work to a pharmacist or to a

00:22:04.530 --> 00:22:08.980
GP, you will qualify for free vaccines as
well, and getting that message out is important.

00:22:08.980 --> 00:22:12.140
Yeah, indeed. Moira?
I was going to make a similar point in relation

00:22:12.140 --> 00:22:23.850
to the social care sector, but I hope that
we will basically pay attention to what the

00:22:23.850 --> 00:22:27.880
level of take-up is in social care. It’s
obviously taken some time to build up even

00:22:27.880 --> 00:22:33.710
that level in the NHS staff, and of course,
there’s a much more disperse staff group,

00:22:33.710 --> 00:22:37.520
who – and the messages, again, should be
thinking about how they would be communicated

00:22:37.520 --> 00:22:43.330
with, as well.
Fantastic. Well, I’m sure we are all looking

00:22:43.330 --> 00:22:46.630
forward to getting our flu jabs, as a Board,
in the very near future.

00:22:46.630 --> 00:22:52.100
Can I just say, last year, the Board of NHS
England had a 100% success rate on flu vaccinations,

00:22:52.100 --> 00:22:57.050
and we’re looking for these behaviours to
be modelled with our new Board.

00:22:57.050 --> 00:22:58.970
Collectively.
We accept the challenge.

00:22:58.970 --> 00:23:02.090
Good, fantastic.
Exactly. I have to say how encouraging it

00:23:02.090 --> 00:23:06.360
is. I know how challenging the circumstances
are, but it is so encouraging to have these

00:23:06.360 --> 00:23:12.950
joint reports and a shared view of our preparation
for winter, our operational performance and

00:23:12.950 --> 00:23:17.140
our financial performance. It’s a huge step
on, actually, even since May, when we last

00:23:17.140 --> 00:23:21.510
all met together. So, thank you.
With that, I will hand over to my co-chair.

00:23:21.510 --> 00:23:25.260
Well, thank you, Dido. The next item on the
agenda is the long-term plan for the NHS.

00:23:25.260 --> 00:23:30.160
And I should just, I think, point out for
those who are observing the meeting that all

00:23:30.160 --> 00:23:35.080
of us this morning have spent some time trying
to get to grips with this and the complexity

00:23:35.080 --> 00:23:41.310
of the workstreams, and the public engagement
and stakeholder engagement processes that

00:23:41.310 --> 00:23:45.220
have been undertaken. So, with that background,
I think it’s very useful to invite Simon

00:23:45.220 --> 00:23:50.800
to introduce the theme, and then if I may,
Ian, turn to you for further comments. But

00:23:50.800 --> 00:23:54.220
Simon, over to you.
Okay, thanks, Malcolm. As you say, we have

00:23:54.220 --> 00:24:00.550
had a chance to have quite a good discussion
on the substantive process and content points.

00:24:00.550 --> 00:24:04.151
So, this morning, this is obviously a work
in progress. So, really, just to say three

00:24:04.151 --> 00:24:07.550
things.
First of all, remind us all that the starting

00:24:07.550 --> 00:24:13.790
point for the long-term plan is an objective
assessment as to how well the NHS is doing

00:24:13.790 --> 00:24:20.060
on clinical quality outcomes and the health
of the population, and we’re working with

00:24:20.060 --> 00:24:25.340
academic partners, Public Health England,
to ensure that we’ve got good, objective

00:24:25.340 --> 00:24:30.610
baselining of that. Many third-party organisations
have produced those kinds of assessments in

00:24:30.610 --> 00:24:36.110
the run-up to the NHS 70th anniversary, but
that helps us frame what the opportunity looks

00:24:36.110 --> 00:24:41.290
like over the next decade.
Secondly, we are starting from a position

00:24:41.290 --> 00:24:46.390
where we’ve got intense financial and operational
pressures in the system, and therefore, we

00:24:46.390 --> 00:24:53.630
need to recognise that those are calls on
resourcing, on workforce, in the early years

00:24:53.630 --> 00:24:58.770
of this plan period.
And thirdly, we recognise that we have begun

00:24:58.770 --> 00:25:03.260
a journey of – towards a more integrated
care delivery system, so we’re not starting

00:25:03.260 --> 00:25:07.640
with a blank sheet of paper here. We’re
being clear about what the milestones to complete

00:25:07.640 --> 00:25:15.350
that task look like, and obviously, Andrew
Morris represents, embodies what that journey

00:25:15.350 --> 00:25:20.520
needs to look like, so we’ve got to reflect
that position as the starting point for the

00:25:20.520 --> 00:25:23.460
plan, as well.
So, clinical outcomes, the financial and operational

00:25:23.460 --> 00:25:27.930
pressures, the journey we’ve been on, where
have we got to. That’s the kind of baseline

00:25:27.930 --> 00:25:32.870
from which we begin the process.
But related to that, we are – we’ve got

00:25:32.870 --> 00:25:37.600
what I would call a sort of trifocal lens.
So, for some of these, if we’re trying to

00:25:37.600 --> 00:25:41.230
think clearly about where we want to be as
a country on our cancer performance, our mental

00:25:41.230 --> 00:25:47.230
health performance, cardiovascular disease,
other big causes of avoidable illness or disability,

00:25:47.230 --> 00:25:52.220
we need to think out over a decade.
The second timeline is, we’ve got five years’

00:25:52.220 --> 00:25:57.500
worth of funding, and we’ve probably got
more financial headroom in the sort of third,

00:25:57.500 --> 00:26:01.520
fourth and potentially fifth years of that
than we have in the first couple of years.

00:26:01.520 --> 00:26:06.100
And that is the third timeline, the here and
now, when we can be much more granular about

00:26:06.100 --> 00:26:13.370
what the trade-offs and the opportunities
look like for 2019/20, 2020/21, so it’s

00:26:13.370 --> 00:26:16.380
kind of ten years, it’s five years, it’s
two years: it’s that trifocal lens that

00:26:16.380 --> 00:26:20.740
we’re bringing to the exercise.
And that then draws me on to who the ‘we’

00:26:20.740 --> 00:26:26.910
is. There has actually been an enormous process
of engagement and mobilisation. I think Ian

00:26:26.910 --> 00:26:34.890
and I want to thank the huge numbers of patients,
organisations, frontline staff, trust/CCG

00:26:34.890 --> 00:26:40.570
leaders, and members of the public who have
involved – been engaged with us so far.

00:26:40.570 --> 00:26:45.600
The sort of first phase of this engagement
process completes at the end of this month,

00:26:45.600 --> 00:26:50.920
where the working groups which we’ve set
out publicly will have developed their sort

00:26:50.920 --> 00:26:57.520
of initial propositions. Then we are using
the period October through the end of November

00:26:57.520 --> 00:27:03.960
to actually shape the sort of cross-cutting
trade-offs, the synergies, the challenge,

00:27:03.960 --> 00:27:08.100
the scrutiny, as to what that looks like ahead
of being able to publish the long-term plan,

00:27:08.100 --> 00:27:13.640
aiming for the end of November. And then the
third phase of the process is that, given

00:27:13.640 --> 00:27:16.440
the trifocal lens, there are things that at
five or ten years we won’t be trying to

00:27:16.440 --> 00:27:22.290
sort of be precise about nationally; there
will need to be a period of local discussion,

00:27:22.290 --> 00:27:27.451
engagement, where the Health Service and our
partners – local government, the voluntary

00:27:27.451 --> 00:27:33.610
sector – between December and April then
make that real for each of the STP geographies,

00:27:33.610 --> 00:27:40.160
and they set out how, within that broad national
framework, they intend to advance the long-term

00:27:40.160 --> 00:27:45.960
plan for their populations.
So, we now know the date of the Budget is

00:27:45.960 --> 00:27:52.060
October 29th. What that means is that the
long-term plan on the – sticking with the

00:27:52.060 --> 00:27:55.640
original timetable which we set out at the
end of November will follow the Budget rather

00:27:55.640 --> 00:28:01.180
than be concurrent with it. But by the time
we get to the end of October, we’re aiming

00:28:01.180 --> 00:28:07.260
to have done a lot of the work to think about
the trade-offs, the resourcing and some of

00:28:07.260 --> 00:28:11.650
the workforce constraints that at least some
of the sort of major themes that might emerge

00:28:11.650 --> 00:28:15.840
from the long-term plan will be available
to us on that sort of timeline.

00:28:15.840 --> 00:28:22.480
Thank you, Simon. Ian?
Completely agree with that set of comments.

00:28:22.480 --> 00:28:30.920
This is something that I think exemplifies
our joint working, really, in the sense that

00:28:30.920 --> 00:28:38.550
this is a joint process and ultimately a joint
product, I think, on behalf of NHS England

00:28:38.550 --> 00:28:43.600
and NHS Improvement. And we are literally
joined at the hip, I think, as we go through

00:28:43.600 --> 00:28:49.929
this really complex process over what is inevitably
quite an aggressive timescale, but also, to

00:28:49.929 --> 00:28:54.030
not miss the opportunity that this plan offers
us.

00:28:54.030 --> 00:28:58.870
And I think something I would add, it’s
a great opportunity, actually, that this is

00:28:58.870 --> 00:29:08.240
the NHS’s plan. So, I think, you know, that
process that Simon outlined is a planning

00:29:08.240 --> 00:29:15.600
process that the NHS, supported by the people
in this room, but the NHS, actually has to

00:29:15.600 --> 00:29:22.440
use to set its own direction. So, this isn’t
a plan that sits in Simon’s office or my

00:29:22.440 --> 00:29:32.240
office and comes out in November for – in
– out of a vacuum; this is a plan that is

00:29:32.240 --> 00:29:38.480
built up with the process of engagement and
involvement at every stage in the process

00:29:38.480 --> 00:29:43.450
that Simon discussed. And I’m particularly
heartened to see that the way we’re building

00:29:43.450 --> 00:29:50.030
this with a series of workstreams, looking
both at the clinical areas where, despite

00:29:50.030 --> 00:29:55.610
offering good care, we aspire – now, we
aspire to do better over the next ten years,

00:29:55.610 --> 00:30:02.470
and by definition, that’s a stretching set
of conversations, and those are going on in

00:30:02.470 --> 00:30:06.860
a number of the workstreams. But there are
other workstreams which, very sensibly, are

00:30:06.860 --> 00:30:12.120
looking at how we actually run the NHS, how
we spend the money, and how we use the organisations

00:30:12.120 --> 00:30:18.100
of the NHS to drive towards our objectives
over the next five years. And in all those

00:30:18.100 --> 00:30:25.480
workstreams, there is very significant engagement
and, indeed, leadership from people out there

00:30:25.480 --> 00:30:30.580
running organisations, from people providing
care to patients across the country, and I

00:30:30.580 --> 00:30:35.780
think that process is critical because that
gives us a sense of confidence that this will

00:30:35.780 --> 00:30:41.560
be the plan from the NHS for the NHS. I think
that’s the first point I’d make.

00:30:41.560 --> 00:30:49.350
I think the second point, just to pick up
on something Simon said and perhaps take it

00:30:49.350 --> 00:30:54.440
on a little bit, this plan for me, you can
describe it in a whole number of different

00:30:54.440 --> 00:31:03.880
ways, but I think it fundamentally has two
key sets of objectives. The first is to say,

00:31:03.880 --> 00:31:10.630
how do we drive the improvements in care that
we know we need to make for our populations

00:31:10.630 --> 00:31:16.750
over a ten-year period, building on the excellent
work, the excellent care that I think everybody

00:31:16.750 --> 00:31:21.600
acknowledges the NHS generally gives now,
but say we’re not satisfied with that? And

00:31:21.600 --> 00:31:26.620
we know in a changing NHS, we want to do better,
and so I think there are a series of – you

00:31:26.620 --> 00:31:32.520
know, of ideas that are going to come from
that, that will set out, I think, a very compelling

00:31:32.520 --> 00:31:38.990
set of objectives that I think will be things
that we will want to sign up to and make happen.

00:31:38.990 --> 00:31:46.290
But at the same time – and I welcome this
second but equally important focus – we

00:31:46.290 --> 00:31:54.310
have 3.4% real-terms growth to use to run
the NHS over the next five years, and that’s

00:31:54.310 --> 00:32:01.090
a significant increase in resourcing. And
it’s incumbent upon us, I think, to use

00:32:01.090 --> 00:32:06.910
that really wisely to deliver the maximum
health gain. And so the second aspect of this

00:32:06.910 --> 00:32:15.360
is that this has to then provide our route
map for running the NHS from the position

00:32:15.360 --> 00:32:21.540
it is today to an even better position at
the end of that five years, recognising that

00:32:21.540 --> 00:32:28.580
we have to deal along the way with the issues
of financial pressure, of workforce challenges,

00:32:28.580 --> 00:32:34.520
as well as the things that we do really well
and we want to do even better. And so the

00:32:34.520 --> 00:32:39.780
fact that there are these two different levels
in the plan I think seems to me to be entirely

00:32:39.780 --> 00:32:44.870
appropriate, and it gives us a hope that at
the end of the first five years, we should

00:32:44.870 --> 00:32:51.890
be able to set out a route to both clinical
and financial sustainability and improvement,

00:32:51.890 --> 00:32:55.850
but also, set that very much in a compelling
vision for where we want to be clinically

00:32:55.850 --> 00:33:01.200
in ten years, and those two things obviously
join together to provide that forward direction

00:33:01.200 --> 00:33:07.260
that I think the NHS is asking for. So, of
course, inevitably there’s a lot of process,

00:33:07.260 --> 00:33:12.490
a lot of discussion, a huge amount of engagement,
and that will involve trade-offs, and we have

00:33:12.490 --> 00:33:17.230
to be part of those discussions and honest
about those discussions. But I’m actually

00:33:17.230 --> 00:33:23.720
very excited about the opportunity we have
to work together to run this planning process,

00:33:23.720 --> 00:33:27.680
and look forward to continuing discussions
with people around this table over the next

00:33:27.680 --> 00:33:31.690
couple of months.
Well, thank you, Ian. Let me invite any comments

00:33:31.690 --> 00:33:41.960
or questions to Simon or Ian. David Roberts?
So, Simon and Ian, you talked about the – I

00:33:41.960 --> 00:33:48.640
think an important trade-off about sort of
the amount of investment that’s available

00:33:48.640 --> 00:33:55.720
to create new versus the making sure that
we have sort of got a stable base. And one

00:33:55.720 --> 00:34:02.460
of the critical components of that, I think,
is, you know, you can’t change an organisation

00:34:02.460 --> 00:34:08.510
without capacity, and part of that capacity
is capital. And if I think about, whether

00:34:08.510 --> 00:34:13.600
it be maintenance backlogs, whether it be
the technology investment that we all believe

00:34:13.600 --> 00:34:22.020
will transform service and efficiency, and
I think about the need to reconfigure health

00:34:22.020 --> 00:34:27.310
systems to be able to deliver to the needs
of an ageing population, for example, more

00:34:27.310 --> 00:34:32.730
in the community, then none of that is going
to happen without capital. And that seems

00:34:32.730 --> 00:34:36.980
to me the missing part of the equation, at
the moment, and therefore, I wonder if you

00:34:36.980 --> 00:34:41.490
could, Simon or Ian, maybe just talk to us
about your thoughts on how you’re going

00:34:41.490 --> 00:34:45.659
to manage that process with government and
the Treasury in particular.

00:34:45.659 --> 00:34:51.500
Yes, certainly. And I would say that’s not
the only missing part of the equation. As

00:34:51.500 --> 00:34:57.070
we know as well, what happens around public
health grants and prevention, what happens

00:34:57.070 --> 00:35:02.960
around the availability of social care for
children and adults, particularly frail, older

00:35:02.960 --> 00:35:07.430
adults, and then of course the workforce training
budgets that are represented through HEE,

00:35:07.430 --> 00:35:11.250
those are kind of four – including the capital
point – those are kind of four dependencies

00:35:11.250 --> 00:35:15.580
which are external.
I think that message has been understood by

00:35:15.580 --> 00:35:20.370
those who decisions it would be to sort those
things out. But the timeline for that: some

00:35:20.370 --> 00:35:27.370
of that will be with the spending review,
and so we’ve got to show the connections

00:35:27.370 --> 00:35:33.290
between what we’re proposing in the plan
and getting good answers on those topics.

00:35:33.290 --> 00:35:42.810
The Prime Minister has been very clear that
the future funding of adult social care should

00:35:42.810 --> 00:35:47.350
at the very least ensure that there are no
additional pressures arising in the National

00:35:47.350 --> 00:35:53.220
Health Service in subsequent years, and so
sort of fleshing out what that means in practice

00:35:53.220 --> 00:35:57.640
and how we work together to bring that about
is – along with the capital point, the training

00:35:57.640 --> 00:36:03.630
linked to the workforce, and the public health
piece – an integral part of this thing flying.

00:36:03.630 --> 00:36:11.190
it’s clearly important that there is dislocation
between ambition and plan from resources.

00:36:11.190 --> 00:36:14.980
Exactly, which is why, when it comes to, for
example, the central activity modelling here,

00:36:14.980 --> 00:36:20.370
we’ve got to kind of connect what is kind
of the sort of trend, what is the opportunity

00:36:20.370 --> 00:36:24.830
cost of trend relative to investment in community
health services, and how might those two kind

00:36:24.830 --> 00:36:29.480
of help balance each other off, and what are
the kind of external pressures flowing into

00:36:29.480 --> 00:36:36.220
the system, of which the pressures in adult
social care have obviously been enormous contributors

00:36:36.220 --> 00:36:41.490
over the last five years.
And just to add to that, the other general

00:36:41.490 --> 00:36:46.790
point which I think is inevitable in a planning
exercise of this magnitude is that this is

00:36:46.790 --> 00:36:55.060
also about prioritisation. I mean, you know,
we start from a – in terms of revenue, certainty

00:36:55.060 --> 00:36:57.430
of where we’re going over the next five
years, and that gives us something to play

00:36:57.430 --> 00:37:01.920
with and to plan for.
But just, whether you’re looking at the

00:37:01.920 --> 00:37:07.330
revenue expenditure or the capital expenditure,
and I think the incumbency on all of us as

00:37:07.330 --> 00:37:13.450
we work with the workstreams and then go through
this process of understanding where the prioritisation

00:37:13.450 --> 00:37:19.580
lies is to be really explicit and clear about
that, and I think picking those areas I think

00:37:19.580 --> 00:37:22.790
will be important. I suppose the question
I thought you were going to ask, because it’s

00:37:22.790 --> 00:37:28.960
another example of where we have to understand
two potentially competing pressures, and probably

00:37:28.960 --> 00:37:35.120
do both of them, we also have to think about
how we not only recognise the need to start

00:37:35.120 --> 00:37:39.550
from where the NHS is and build financial
sustainability back in, but create enough

00:37:39.550 --> 00:37:42.440
headroom for transformation. Because across
the –

00:37:42.440 --> 00:37:44.240
And that might flow up –
I thought it might be, David. And I thought

00:37:44.240 --> 00:37:49.030
I’d get out there now, because, I mean,
the reality is, over the next five years,

00:37:49.030 --> 00:37:54.020
we need to use the new money for both creating
– we’ve got to get to a financially sustainable

00:37:54.020 --> 00:37:59.460
NHS, including a financially sustainable provider
sector within that. And indeed, the paper

00:37:59.460 --> 00:38:05.800
refers to the financial tests that are essential
on this. But at the same time, simply by carrying

00:38:05.800 --> 00:38:11.370
on doing what we’re doing, we will be probably
passing away the future, so we do have to

00:38:11.370 --> 00:38:15.230
also have that difficult conversation about
ensuring that there’s some scope for real

00:38:15.230 --> 00:38:18.730
transformation here, as well, and clearly,
some of that transformation will be linked

00:38:18.730 --> 00:38:23.250
with productivity and efficiency, and should
actually therefore help us balance the books,

00:38:23.250 --> 00:38:27.670
but at the same time, you often need that
transformation money earlier in the process

00:38:27.670 --> 00:38:31.450
than at the end of it. So, all of those things
are inevitably concerns that we’ll have

00:38:31.450 --> 00:38:36.400
to deal with. The key is having a clear objective
that we probably have to do both of those

00:38:36.400 --> 00:38:39.070
things.
Ruth and Michelle. Ruth?

00:38:39.070 --> 00:38:44.400
Thank you. I’m delighted that we’ve got
workforce being considered as part of the

00:38:44.400 --> 00:38:48.190
long-term plan. I think it was perhaps something
that was missed from the Five Year Forward

00:38:48.190 --> 00:38:53.550
View, so it’s a really great opportunity
to have it. And Ian, Jim and Navina and myself

00:38:53.550 --> 00:38:59.250
are working hard to engage with a whole range
of people, and I’m very grateful indeed

00:38:59.250 --> 00:39:05.210
for all the people that are sending me lots
of emails and – our opportunity to hear

00:39:05.210 --> 00:39:11.050
from them.
So, the big message is about what we’re

00:39:11.050 --> 00:39:17.600
trying to do to develop, about how we capture
the heart of the NHS and the heart of the

00:39:17.600 --> 00:39:21.430
workforce, and the key actions that are then
going to move the dial. So, how we support

00:39:21.430 --> 00:39:28.570
our staff, how we address the supply of our
staff, and how we use technology are really

00:39:28.570 --> 00:39:35.500
important aspects then to support our finances,
our quality, our urgent care. So, getting

00:39:35.500 --> 00:39:40.940
the sufficient workforce is going to underpin
all the rest of the workstreams. We can’t

00:39:40.940 --> 00:39:45.390
have fantastic respiratory care or cardiovascular
care or mental health unless we’ve got the

00:39:45.390 --> 00:39:52.590
right workforce in the right numbers and with
the right skills and experience.

00:39:52.590 --> 00:39:57.820
But I guess the – going back to what David’s
point is, it’s – these things are – these

00:39:57.820 --> 00:40:02.520
costs associated to this that we’re going
to need to utilise what money we’ve got

00:40:02.520 --> 00:40:06.840
better, as well as considerations for priority
of resourcing in the future.

00:40:06.840 --> 00:40:10.450
Thank you. Michelle?
Thank you. Simon and Ian, it’s been a very

00:40:10.450 --> 00:40:20.000
welcome [BREAK IN AUDIO] in the design of
the initial stage of engagement for the long-term

00:40:20.000 --> 00:40:25.430
plan. Could you give us a sense of how that’s
going, and what do you think some of the key

00:40:25.430 --> 00:40:28.810
issues are at the moment emerging through
that work, and what could it potentially mean

00:40:28.810 --> 00:40:29.810
for some of our most disadvantaged communities
and people?

00:40:29.810 --> 00:40:39.690
I’m happy for Simon to kick off, I think.
Okay. Well, I don’t want to second-guess

00:40:39.690 --> 00:40:43.590
what the group on prevention and inequalities
are going to come up; they’re obviously

00:40:43.590 --> 00:40:48.530
in the process of kind of refining their propositions.
But I do know based on conversations with

00:40:48.530 --> 00:40:54.670
them so far that first of all, there’s a
sort of question mark about what’s happening

00:40:54.670 --> 00:41:00.960
to changes in life expectancy across the UK
and across a number of industrialised countries.

00:41:00.960 --> 00:41:06.040
There are some data that ONS have published
within the last week on that, showing differences

00:41:06.040 --> 00:41:09.980
within the UK, and there will shortly – I
don’t think – I’m not going to break

00:41:09.980 --> 00:41:15.000
the embargo, but there’s a very important
paper coming out in the Lancet in a few weeks’

00:41:15.000 --> 00:41:19.690
time, which is going to decompose this by
different areas of England, and it’s got

00:41:19.690 --> 00:41:24.170
some quite – well, I’d better not say
any more or I’m going to be shot at this

00:41:24.170 --> 00:41:28.040
point. But anyway, we have access to those
– early access to those data, and they’re

00:41:28.040 --> 00:41:32.340
helping inform what the programme would look
like. So, we’ve obviously got decisions

00:41:32.340 --> 00:41:36.430
to make around resource allocation, we’ve
got decisions to make around whether we build

00:41:36.430 --> 00:41:42.150
in intentionally specific inequalities, reducing
goals across the board and into the particular

00:41:42.150 --> 00:41:47.460
sort of clinical programmes that we’re developing.
And then frankly, we’ve got – as we’ve

00:41:47.460 --> 00:41:52.750
discussed around these tables before – we’ve
got a series of questions about whether our

00:41:52.750 --> 00:42:00.980
actual delivery mechanisms are too undifferentiated
to really make a difference for some of the

00:42:00.980 --> 00:42:04.870
types of access inequalities that we see.
I mean, we hypothesise that the answer to

00:42:04.870 --> 00:42:07.600
that question is yes, and therefore we’ve
posed the challenge, so what would you actually

00:42:07.600 --> 00:42:12.160
need to do differently in each of those delivery
chains if you were going to get at that inequality

00:42:12.160 --> 00:42:15.950
reduction.
So, those are the sorts of ways that the group

00:42:15.950 --> 00:42:19.660
is conceptualising it. Part of the October
process is then getting them to challenge

00:42:19.660 --> 00:42:23.780
all of the other workstreams around whether
they’re being sufficiently radical and whether

00:42:23.780 --> 00:42:28.780
their way of thinking about the inequality
reduction is kind of going to work. And we’re

00:42:28.780 --> 00:42:34.070
doing this very closely with Public Health
England and with many other partners, as well.

00:42:34.070 --> 00:42:36.990
Thank you.
Fine. Well, thank you very much. I don’t

00:42:36.990 --> 00:42:42.230
see any other requests. I mean, I think we
have to recall that this is an immensely complex

00:42:42.230 --> 00:42:46.710
operation, and we’re all immensely impressed
by the work and the effort that’s gone into

00:42:46.710 --> 00:42:52.480
the workstreams, and I’d like to actually,
on behalf of our two boards, extend our thanks

00:42:52.480 --> 00:42:56.630
to all of our colleagues in our own institutions
who are putting so much time and effort into

00:42:56.630 --> 00:43:00.620
this. I’ve had an estimate that some people
are putting, you know, as much as 50% of their

00:43:00.620 --> 00:43:06.290
time, and that’s already working 150% of
their time. And only with that effort are

00:43:06.290 --> 00:43:11.100
we ever likely to succeed.
I think it’s sobering to realise that actually,

00:43:11.100 --> 00:43:15.560
in this room is the bulk of the national leadership
of one of the greatest institutions in the

00:43:15.560 --> 00:43:20.670
country, if not the world. But I use the word
‘institution’ in a figurative rather than

00:43:20.670 --> 00:43:24.110
a literal sense; it’s not a single institution,
and it’s not somewhere where we can sit

00:43:24.110 --> 00:43:28.200
and push buttons and pull levers and things
happen. It’s an immensely complex ecosystem,

00:43:28.200 --> 00:43:33.500
and much of what we do through the long-term
plan has to inspire and has to lead people

00:43:33.500 --> 00:43:38.470
to a new vision of how the future could be
delivered for the patients and the population

00:43:38.470 --> 00:43:44.310
that we serve. And as the great wave of enthusiasm
hits the hard rock of resources and subsides

00:43:44.310 --> 00:43:49.170
into the sands of delivery, so we’ve –
Wow. Is that Tennyson? Florid.

00:43:49.170 --> 00:43:59.360
– got ourselves an immensely complex problem.
But I hope the Board feels comfortable that

00:43:59.360 --> 00:44:04.900
actually, work is in hand to deliver something
which will be of the quality that we need,

00:44:04.900 --> 00:44:09.330
and we will be able to engage all members
of the Board through the coming six to eight

00:44:09.330 --> 00:44:13.700
weeks as we bring this to final fruition.
But thank you, again, for all of those who

00:44:13.700 --> 00:44:16.760
have been working so hard on this front. Dido,
back to you.

00:44:16.760 --> 00:44:20.970
Goodness, this co-chairing business is getting
quite tough, Malcolm, competing – rather

00:44:20.970 --> 00:44:26.480
more prosaically, we’ll hand over. Our next
item on the agenda is an update on progress

00:44:26.480 --> 00:44:30.700
on the integrated care systems programme.
Ben, are you going to take us through.

00:44:30.700 --> 00:44:36.220
Yes, quite happy to say a few words. The paper
you’ve got in front of you is partly an

00:44:36.220 --> 00:44:41.870
opportunity, as you can see, to give an update
on the progress that our now 14 integrated

00:44:41.870 --> 00:44:46.180
care systems are making. But I think perhaps
more importantly, an opportunity just to give

00:44:46.180 --> 00:44:51.120
a sense of how we think this connects to the
work on the long-term plan that Simon, Ian

00:44:51.120 --> 00:44:56.250
and others have just talked through.
As you know, at the heart of the idea of integrated

00:44:56.250 --> 00:45:02.490
care systems is the very basic principle that
if we are to – if we’re to really get

00:45:02.490 --> 00:45:06.220
to the heart of how we do things like improve
the quality of care for frail older people,

00:45:06.220 --> 00:45:12.280
for people with long-term mental health conditions,
and so forth, we simply can’t do that by

00:45:12.280 --> 00:45:18.420
looking at the world through purely institutional
siloes. We need colleagues from across general

00:45:18.420 --> 00:45:22.790
practice, community and mental health services,
adult social care and acute services, across

00:45:22.790 --> 00:45:28.670
providers and commissioners, to come together
and essentially make a more common, consistent

00:45:28.670 --> 00:45:31.700
set of solutions about how they use their
collective resources, and I don’t just mean

00:45:31.700 --> 00:45:40.090
financial resources, but workforce, estates,
other assets. How they design and oversee

00:45:40.090 --> 00:45:44.880
delivery of care and support services, and
how all of that translates into better population

00:45:44.880 --> 00:45:47.030
health.
What we’ve been doing, as you can see, I

00:45:47.030 --> 00:45:53.060
hope, from the paper, is working with both
that initial group of 14 integrated care systems

00:45:53.060 --> 00:45:59.380
and with a wide range of other partners to
really try and distil what the sort of essential

00:45:59.380 --> 00:46:04.380
characteristics of integrated care systems
are. And I’d really draw colleagues’ attention

00:46:04.380 --> 00:46:10.300
to paragraph seven of this paper. The framework
here isn’t something that we’ve – it’s

00:46:10.300 --> 00:46:16.170
not a sort of blueprint that we’ve invented
nationally: this has emerged pretty much organically

00:46:16.170 --> 00:46:20.290
from the work that those integrated care systems
have done. And I think the significance of

00:46:20.290 --> 00:46:26.360
it is that, when it comes down to it, most
of the work involved in this new approach

00:46:26.360 --> 00:46:30.980
to resource planning, services and population
health, most of it happens at the level either

00:46:30.980 --> 00:46:37.881
of quite small neighbourhoods that sort of
coalesce around primary care networks, or

00:46:37.881 --> 00:46:43.270
places covering broadly populations of 200,000,
300,000, 400,000, 500,000, the precise numbers

00:46:43.270 --> 00:46:48.310
don’t matter. What does matter is that within
those populations, you’ve got people coming

00:46:48.310 --> 00:46:56.340
together to really think about how you integrate
care around the needs of particular groups

00:46:56.340 --> 00:47:01.690
of service users.
And part of the reason we think this is so

00:47:01.690 --> 00:47:05.730
important and why integrated care systems
think it’s so important, is that by starting

00:47:05.730 --> 00:47:10.460
to describe the practical activities that
are carried out at those different levels,

00:47:10.460 --> 00:47:15.960
this really starts to speak much more to people
working in clinical teams, and it also speaks

00:47:15.960 --> 00:47:21.870
much better to local government and to the
type of deeper, stronger partnerships that

00:47:21.870 --> 00:47:26.200
we need to forge with local government, both
to address wider determinants of health and

00:47:26.200 --> 00:47:30.461
health inequalities, and also to provide better
care for people with complex health and care

00:47:30.461 --> 00:47:32.970
needs.
At the same time, as you’ll see from paragraph

00:47:32.970 --> 00:47:40.820
seven, we and colleagues in the Service developing
this approach do think it’s important to

00:47:40.820 --> 00:47:45.310
have some things done at the bigger, system
level, in other words, broadly speaking, populations

00:47:45.310 --> 00:47:50.660
of about a million plus. Partly because there
are some decisions, for instance, about how

00:47:50.660 --> 00:47:57.930
you plan more specialist acute services, potentially
some big decisions about how you manage common

00:47:57.930 --> 00:48:05.290
workforce issues, potentially some big population
health interventions. And also, a sort of

00:48:05.290 --> 00:48:10.980
confidence that together, you are sort of
best managing the overall NHS resources for

00:48:10.980 --> 00:48:16.340
that patch that are best done at that system
level. But all our emerging integrated care

00:48:16.340 --> 00:48:22.540
systems are following something like that
approach.

00:48:22.540 --> 00:48:29.220
Just the other thing to add is that, I won’t
pre-empt the outcomes of the work we’re

00:48:29.220 --> 00:48:37.780
doing in support of the long-term plan on
how we put integrated care systems on a firmer

00:48:37.780 --> 00:48:42.440
and more consistent footing around the country.
What I will say is that, from all the engagement

00:48:42.440 --> 00:48:47.190
we’ve done so far, two very clear messages
come through. The first is that, when you

00:48:47.190 --> 00:48:52.570
start to describe the work that integrated
care systems are doing in these terms, it

00:48:52.570 --> 00:48:57.960
kind of becomes obvious that everywhere should
be doing this, and we are rightly being pushed

00:48:57.960 --> 00:49:05.600
to do more to avoid this being seen as an
optional extra. We mustn’t run for too much

00:49:05.600 --> 00:49:10.920
longer with what appears to too many people
to be a kind of two-tier system. Everybody

00:49:10.920 --> 00:49:15.190
should be doing this.
And the second and I thought very consistent

00:49:15.190 --> 00:49:21.770
message is that, in all of that, it’s really
important to make clear that these things

00:49:21.770 --> 00:49:29.250
called integrated care systems are collaborations
between people who provide health and care

00:49:29.250 --> 00:49:34.480
in different settings, whether that’s general
practice, mental health services, acute services,

00:49:34.480 --> 00:49:39.220
and so on and so forth. And none of this undermines
– in fact, all of it reinforces the importance

00:49:39.220 --> 00:49:45.010
of having strong provider organisations, but
in some ways, our definition of a strong provider

00:49:45.010 --> 00:49:49.880
organisation is evolving. It is no longer
enough simply to provide high-quality care

00:49:49.880 --> 00:49:57.030
for your direct patients and to be good stewards
of your – of the public resource that you

00:49:57.030 --> 00:50:03.480
manage; a strong, successful provider, whether
it’s a network of GP practices or a hospital

00:50:03.480 --> 00:50:08.280
or mental health trust, or a community trust,
a strong provider also works alongside its

00:50:08.280 --> 00:50:14.930
community partners to do all the things set
out here. So, much more, I hope, will come

00:50:14.930 --> 00:50:19.750
from our – from the work we’re doing in
support of the long-term plan, but I think

00:50:19.750 --> 00:50:23.410
those are the two big messages coming through
so far.

00:50:23.410 --> 00:50:28.360
Thank you. I wonder if I could start by asking
Andrew to comment on the paper, given your

00:50:28.360 --> 00:50:37.010
specific experience, and then go on to Joanne.
Okay. So, I lead the Frimley system. It’s

00:50:37.010 --> 00:50:44.490
got about 800,000 residents. It spends about
£1.4 billion on health and social care. And

00:50:44.490 --> 00:50:51.540
we’ve developed a coalition of the willing
in terms of all the leaders in local authorities

00:50:51.540 --> 00:50:58.180
and local health organisations have signed
up to a joint vision and a joint plan around

00:50:58.180 --> 00:51:03.850
how we provide better integrated care that’s
closer to people’s homes. So, we’re working

00:51:03.850 --> 00:51:10.140
on three big initiatives that we’ve got
evidence work. So, one is the integrated care

00:51:10.140 --> 00:51:16.480
teams. Hospitals can only do a finite amount
of activity, so can we push more work into

00:51:16.480 --> 00:51:21.871
the community. So, we’ve got teams of nurses,
with some mental health, social work support,

00:51:21.871 --> 00:51:26.270
some consultant support where appropriate,
looking after the high-risk patients who the

00:51:26.270 --> 00:51:33.310
GPs think are likely to bounce into hospital.
And they’re providing more upstream care

00:51:33.310 --> 00:51:39.410
and keeping people in their homes for a lot
longer. So, if you look at the number of emergency

00:51:39.410 --> 00:51:45.510
admissions into Frimley Park – because we
wrapped this service around one of the two

00:51:45.510 --> 00:51:50.510
DJHs in our patch – admissions – emergency
admissions for one night or more have remained

00:51:50.510 --> 00:51:57.460
flat for the last three years. And that’s
taken a load of pressure off the team at the

00:51:57.460 --> 00:52:04.260
front door. We’ve also put more of the decision-makers
on the front door, more consultants, to plug

00:52:04.260 --> 00:52:10.020
into that service and make the right call
around whether we admit or discharge people.

00:52:10.020 --> 00:52:13.970
So, that’s one initiative.
Another one has been around trying to develop

00:52:13.970 --> 00:52:22.650
the 8 A.M. to 8 P.M. offering for general
practice, and that’s slowly being taken

00:52:22.650 --> 00:52:29.160
up in our patch. We’re having to publicise
it a bit more, but it’s there. We’ve got

00:52:29.160 --> 00:52:34.410
an offering from Monday to Friday, and in
some places, some of the practices have been

00:52:34.410 --> 00:52:39.650
really courageous and, you know, tried a Saturday
offering as well, and hopefully we’ll work

00:52:39.650 --> 00:52:46.560
towards a Sunday service. And again, that
just takes a bit of pressure off some of the

00:52:46.560 --> 00:52:53.140
kind of relatively minor conditions that the
two emergency departments look after.

00:52:53.140 --> 00:53:00.200
The third one is around mental health services,
and we’ve had big success with the Safe

00:53:00.200 --> 00:53:07.680
Haven Café that we’ve got in Aldershot.
Aldershot’s quite a deprived area. And any

00:53:07.680 --> 00:53:12.940
resident can just drop in, it’s an evening
service, and have a conversation around their

00:53:12.940 --> 00:53:20.360
concerns. And that’s taken about 30% of
activity away from the A&amp;E, and we got about

00:53:20.360 --> 00:53:26.310
5,000 attendances in its first year. And,
you know, we’ve done a little survey, and

00:53:26.310 --> 00:53:31.740
the satisfaction rate that’s come back is
about 85% who thought it was superb, and a

00:53:31.740 --> 00:53:36.560
much better experience than going to the Frimley
Park A&amp;E, even though the Frimley Park A&amp;E

00:53:36.560 --> 00:53:41.640
is good.
So, those three things I think are making

00:53:41.640 --> 00:53:48.220
a difference, and we’re trying to roll those
out right across our patch. So, in East Berkshire,

00:53:48.220 --> 00:53:55.570
we haven’t got each of those three measures
in place, and that’s an area of focus. We’re

00:53:55.570 --> 00:53:59.480
also looking at a different way of working
with social care. So, we’ve got a paucity

00:53:59.480 --> 00:54:05.730
of capacity for dementia patients, so the
NHS is putting a bit of capital in, Bracknell

00:54:05.730 --> 00:54:11.420
Forest Council’s put a bit of capital in,
and we’re going to provide a 70-bed nursing

00:54:11.420 --> 00:54:18.900
home facility for dementia patients. So, you
will be assessed not at Wexham Park Hospital,

00:54:18.900 --> 00:54:23.960
nor Frimley; you will go into that setting,
and we think will provide a better service

00:54:23.960 --> 00:54:32.660
and will have a better chance of getting people
back home rather than, you know, offering

00:54:32.660 --> 00:54:40.520
a kind of institutional care option, which
often is more expensive. And the big thing

00:54:40.520 --> 00:54:46.600
that, you know, I believe will throw out lots
of savings, I hope, is around the shared care

00:54:46.600 --> 00:54:56.840
record, and how we lever IT and intelligence,
clinical intelligence that comes from it,

00:54:56.840 --> 00:55:01.540
so that we avoid duplication and we speed
up care where it’s appropriate to do so.

00:55:01.540 --> 00:55:07.380
So, I’m quite energised by some of the changes
we’ve dropped in. It’s not easy. I’ve

00:55:07.380 --> 00:55:12.640
got no power. This is a coalition of the willing,
so there are some weeks I think, ‘Gosh,

00:55:12.640 --> 00:55:16.850
we’re going backwards’, but generally
speaking, we’re moving in the right direction.

00:55:16.850 --> 00:55:22.360
And the clinicians are energised, because
we’re saying to them, ‘You design the

00:55:22.360 --> 00:55:27.460
service’. So, I had the privilege of going
to a discussion on heart failure, and for

00:55:27.460 --> 00:55:32.310
the first time ever, some of the consultants
who have been very reticent about changing

00:55:32.310 --> 00:55:37.880
pathways were really up for, you know, let’s
not have so many follow-ups, let’s try and

00:55:37.880 --> 00:55:47.230
move to a different type of service where
we let go a bit and – you know, and try

00:55:47.230 --> 00:55:50.640
and give – you know, empower the patients
to look after themselves rather than, we keep

00:55:50.640 --> 00:55:55.840
pursuing them for follow-up appointments that
have little kind of additional value.

00:55:55.840 --> 00:56:01.980
So, we’re trying to create a big movement.
We’ve got an academy, leadership academy,

00:56:01.980 --> 00:56:08.109
that we put a lot of time and effort into
this. So, this is not just about the top leaders

00:56:08.109 --> 00:56:12.830
having a kind of vision; it’s about, how
can we create a whole movement where people

00:56:12.830 --> 00:56:18.410
feel they’re empowered to make changes on
the deck, and those changes are meaningful

00:56:18.410 --> 00:56:24.369
in terms of improving patient experience and
outcomes? And over time, as a system, we want

00:56:24.369 --> 00:56:31.500
to move to a more of an outcome-delivered
service. So, can we make a big impact, before

00:56:31.500 --> 00:56:36.710
Simon makes the challenge, about improving
mortality in Slough? Because it is very, very

00:56:36.710 --> 00:56:42.109
different to, you know, if you live in Camberley;
there’s six years’ difference. And I think,

00:56:42.109 --> 00:56:48.250
over time, we can. And I’m hoping that the
NHS ten-year plan will give us that freedom

00:56:48.250 --> 00:56:53.760
and opportunity, so we can tackle some of
the deficits around cancer. Because if you

00:56:53.760 --> 00:56:59.100
live in Slough, the outcomes aren’t as good:
nowhere near as good as they are in even Aldershot,

00:56:59.100 --> 00:57:03.530
which is our next kind of deprived area.
So, we’ve got a lot of work to do, and I

00:57:03.530 --> 00:57:08.460
think liberating the clinicians will give
us a better Health Service if we get them

00:57:08.460 --> 00:57:12.200
the opportunity to redesign care.
Thank you. Joanne?

00:57:12.200 --> 00:57:19.590
Thank you. I mean, it’s so encouraging and
exciting to hear about the tangible changes

00:57:19.590 --> 00:57:25.330
that are being made through this approach.
I have a sort of more prosaic question, really,

00:57:25.330 --> 00:57:33.411
about the alignment of governance with the
delivery of real service to real people. Because

00:57:33.411 --> 00:57:39.830
we know that these new integrated care systems
don’t fit perfectly with the organisational

00:57:39.830 --> 00:57:46.260
structure of the NHS that we’re living with
now, and I speak to a lot of Clinical Commissioning

00:57:46.260 --> 00:57:55.930
Group audit chairs, some of whom say to me,
‘We have a legal accountability for commissioning

00:57:55.930 --> 00:58:01.900
decisions and for the way that the money is
spent, and we are not sure how that reconciles

00:58:01.900 --> 00:58:07.480
with that lay governance of the integrated
care systems’. So, you know, you’re very

00:58:07.480 --> 00:58:11.550
much aware of that as a challenge, I know,
and I wondered if you could say a bit about,

00:58:11.550 --> 00:58:16.690
you know, how you envisage we might move towards
better alignment of that accountability on

00:58:16.690 --> 00:58:23.619
behalf of local populations with the increasing
sort of dynamism and the engine of integrated

00:58:23.619 --> 00:58:29.860
care systems.
So, if I may respond, because I think it’s

00:58:29.860 --> 00:58:36.600
a good challenge. So, we’re working to a
system control total, and if someone overspends,

00:58:36.600 --> 00:58:43.650
if an organisation overspends – a CCG or
a provider – someone has to underspend by

00:58:43.650 --> 00:58:49.940
a compensating amount. That’s created a
bit of tension, where you’ve got, you know,

00:58:49.940 --> 00:58:55.050
specific issues, but we get through them,
and I think everyone’s signed up to that

00:58:55.050 --> 00:59:01.150
as a principle. So, that’s, if you like,
a check in the system around, you know, keeping

00:59:01.150 --> 00:59:06.250
control.
We’ve also got a system-wide non-exec and

00:59:06.250 --> 00:59:13.550
lay group who act as a kind of quasi-audit
committee. So, they look at our objectives

00:59:13.550 --> 00:59:18.940
for this year, and, you know, take a view
about, are we making tangible progress against

00:59:18.940 --> 00:59:24.190
those objectives that we’ve set as a system.
And obviously, they’re very interested in

00:59:24.190 --> 00:59:30.680
all the dynamics around the system control
total. So, we’ve set this group up this

00:59:30.680 --> 00:59:36.490
year. We’re in the kind of – still in
the learning kind of stages. And the challenge

00:59:36.490 --> 00:59:43.500
around, you know, the governance piece, particularly
where you’ve got agreement in principle

00:59:43.500 --> 00:59:49.770
from chief execs to do a bit of trading in
terms of one overspends and the other one

00:59:49.770 --> 00:59:54.240
underspends, and how we get respective audit
committees and boards on side, because that’s

00:59:54.240 --> 00:59:59.240
obviously a – you know, a point of tension,
potentially. So, we’re – again, this is

00:59:59.240 --> 01:00:03.510
– you know, we’re in the kind of early
stages of this, but we recognise that that’s

01:00:03.510 --> 01:00:04.510
a legitimate challenge.
Steve?

01:00:04.510 --> 01:00:05.510
Yeah, so I think Andrew’s given a really
powerful illustration of integrated care systems,

01:00:05.510 --> 01:00:16.180
just the transformation of what it can do
when it really gets going. And I had the privilege

01:00:16.180 --> 01:00:22.650
of joining Andrew and his senior clinical
leaders for an evening event in the late spring,

01:00:22.650 --> 01:00:28.680
and I was incredibly impressed at the energy
and the work and the projects that were underway

01:00:28.680 --> 01:00:35.460
between secondary care and primary care and
other clinicians in the room. And I think

01:00:35.460 --> 01:00:42.200
it brings home to us just the importance,
as you said, of liberating clinicians to find

01:00:42.200 --> 01:00:49.370
solutions, because our clinicians have those
solutions, and often feel constrained in bringing

01:00:49.370 --> 01:00:53.600
those forward and developing. Even in Frimley,
in that evening, I detected a little bit of

01:00:53.600 --> 01:00:58.780
reticence about whether they were really being
liberated to do it, which they were. And it

01:00:58.780 --> 01:01:02.950
sounds as if they’re well on that journey
now. So – and when I say clinicians, it’s

01:01:02.950 --> 01:01:07.730
not doctors, and it’s not even doctors and
nurses; it’s doctors, nurses, it’s pharmacists,

01:01:07.730 --> 01:01:11.250
it’s allied healthcare professionals and
the solutions amongst all those clinicians.

01:01:11.250 --> 01:01:17.950
So, I think all the clinicians around this
table would say very clearly to our clinical

01:01:17.950 --> 01:01:24.220
colleagues: this is the opportunity for you
to come with those solutions in an environment

01:01:24.220 --> 01:01:30.820
such as Andrew and other integrated care systems
are creating, and design and generate the

01:01:30.820 --> 01:01:36.470
NHS of the future. And that is a key underpinning
of the approach that’s being taken in the

01:01:36.470 --> 01:01:39.950
long-term plan.
Matthew, you wanted to respond?

01:01:39.950 --> 01:01:48.040
Yes, just two points. One is, the extent to
which this is simultaneously an organisation

01:01:48.040 --> 01:01:52.500
development programme and a – how we’re
going to do performance management and delivery

01:01:52.500 --> 01:01:59.780
in the future simultaneously is – I think
is unusual, and the organisations that have

01:01:59.780 --> 01:02:06.160
come together as the ICSs have been addressing
those issues and how they actually deliver

01:02:06.160 --> 01:02:09.980
change, and I think the point that Ben made
about, at some point, we’re going to need

01:02:09.980 --> 01:02:13.869
to tell people, ‘This is where you’re
going to’, so that – because there are

01:02:13.869 --> 01:02:19.140
difficult things you have to address to get
it up and running’. This sharing of accountability,

01:02:19.140 --> 01:02:24.410
the bringing together, the agreeing to cover
for each other’s financial challenges and

01:02:24.410 --> 01:02:30.960
work collaboratively, is a difficult change
to go through. Our best organisations, our

01:02:30.960 --> 01:02:35.780
best systems, got there quite quickly. It
is good to see that we have got a whole group

01:02:35.780 --> 01:02:41.040
who are following after that, but I – we
have some of the country who need the clarity

01:02:41.040 --> 01:02:45.590
of where this is going to end up in order
to bring everybody to the table to solve this,

01:02:45.590 --> 01:02:51.500
so I think it is important.
The other thing that I’d just briefly say

01:02:51.500 --> 01:02:58.530
is, I think this programme is addressing one
of the NHS’s perennial failures, which is

01:02:58.530 --> 01:03:03.050
how you do spread, how you get innovation
spread to other organisations. And the organisations

01:03:03.050 --> 01:03:07.480
that are sitting around the table, the ICSs
sitting around the table, are also – kind

01:03:07.480 --> 01:03:12.460
of recognise their responsibility to solve
problems on behalf of the rest of the NHS,

01:03:12.460 --> 01:03:19.410
and support that delivery to the rest of the
NHS. And I think using that as a mechanism

01:03:19.410 --> 01:03:25.030
for getting the early adopters and kind of
– in a way, places like Andrew’s are always

01:03:25.030 --> 01:03:28.300
the first to go and do stuff – to actually
help places who aren’t always the first

01:03:28.300 --> 01:03:31.830
to go and do stuff do it as well, because
in the end, we need to cover 50 million people

01:03:31.830 --> 01:03:38.000
like this, not just the, I don’t know, third
of the population we currently have.

01:03:38.000 --> 01:03:43.650
I think there’s something that Andrew said
about creating a movement, and having spent

01:03:43.650 --> 01:03:48.660
all morning together, I think we’re part
of that movement, and it’s really important

01:03:48.660 --> 01:03:54.240
that we are role modelling from the centre
the behaviours, that empowering of patients

01:03:54.240 --> 01:03:58.980
and of all of our staff, and I think that’s
one of the flavours that, as we set clearer,

01:03:58.980 --> 01:04:05.820
you know, terms of engagement, actually, you
can’t substitute the willingness to collaborate

01:04:05.820 --> 01:04:09.359
and to be part of that movement with some
rules; you actually need both, and it’s

01:04:09.359 --> 01:04:13.900
really encouraging to see that coming through.
Brilliant.

01:04:13.900 --> 01:04:16.770
With that, Malcolm, I’ll hand over to you.
Well, that’s a nice segue into the next

01:04:16.770 --> 01:04:22.270
item, which is, how do we model that ourselves?
What is it that we do to bring these two organisations

01:04:22.270 --> 01:04:26.890
together, not by way of any formal merger,
which is not legally permissible, but actually,

01:04:26.890 --> 01:04:34.090
to bring into sharp focus, joined up together,
the whole panoply of commissioning and provision

01:04:34.090 --> 01:04:38.830
across the NHS in England. So, there are two
papers that have been brought to the Board

01:04:38.830 --> 01:04:42.180
this afternoon. I think the second one I’d
like to take pretty much as read, which is

01:04:42.180 --> 01:04:45.830
around what the governance structures would
look like, and we’ve hammered those out

01:04:45.830 --> 01:04:49.760
pretty much between the non-executives. But
the first paper, which I’m going to invite

01:04:49.760 --> 01:04:54.070
Emily to speak to, is to do with how we develop
a common culture between the two organisations

01:04:54.070 --> 01:04:57.000
and how we drive change that brings us together.
Emily?

01:04:57.000 --> 01:05:04.260
Thank you, Malcolm. So, a few words in – to
set up this paper. I think it comes at the

01:05:04.260 --> 01:05:10.090
right point in the meeting, because very much
what we are doing in the joint working programme

01:05:10.090 --> 01:05:15.880
is to change the way that the centre of the
NHS works in order to – in order so that

01:05:15.880 --> 01:05:21.710
we can provide improved support and challenge
to the rest of the NHS through how we work

01:05:21.710 --> 01:05:28.230
with STPs and ICSs and devolved systems, to
which we remain fully committed. So, this

01:05:28.230 --> 01:05:33.590
is all about making sure we reflect what we
need to do for the rest of the NHS and make

01:05:33.590 --> 01:05:35.830
sure we’re providing the best possible support
into that.

01:05:35.830 --> 01:05:41.590
So, over the summer, since we last discussed
this, we’ve made progress on all of the

01:05:41.590 --> 01:05:47.760
five main areas of work that are outlined
in the paper, which are outlined in paragraph

01:05:47.760 --> 01:05:56.201
23. That is, on the operating model, on the
culture and engagement that needs to be in

01:05:56.201 --> 01:06:03.410
place to make that operating model work. On
our finance systems and our responsibilities

01:06:03.410 --> 01:06:10.430
around efficiency. On HR and consultation,
so that we make sure we work with our people

01:06:10.430 --> 01:06:16.750
to make this happen. And indeed, on implementation.
And so, over the summer, we’ve shifted the

01:06:16.750 --> 01:06:20.369
structure of the work in the working programme
to make sure we’ve got that strong focus

01:06:20.369 --> 01:06:26.070
on how we actually make this operating model
operational, focused on the go-live activity,

01:06:26.070 --> 01:06:33.540
whilst we maintain the right grip on the business
as usual and making sure we’re dealing with

01:06:33.540 --> 01:06:38.340
the operational and financial challenges that
we started the meeting with. And obviously,

01:06:38.340 --> 01:06:42.869
all of that is in the context of the long-term
plan, and our work’s very strongly aligned

01:06:42.869 --> 01:06:45.690
with the work of that team, because this is
the operating model that’s going to help

01:06:45.690 --> 01:06:50.619
deliver what that team’s coming up with.
What that means in terms of how this work

01:06:50.619 --> 01:06:55.190
is progressing is, we’re trying to answer
three questions simultaneously and kind of

01:06:55.190 --> 01:07:01.190
do it in mid-air while we support the NHS
every day. The first question is, what are

01:07:01.190 --> 01:07:06.160
we for, as a combined entity? The second question
is, where are we going? And that’s obviously

01:07:06.160 --> 01:07:10.840
work the long-term plan’s doing for the
whole of the NHS; it has relevance for us

01:07:10.840 --> 01:07:16.220
as a joint enterprise. And how will we get
there. So, I think in our discussions, we

01:07:16.220 --> 01:07:19.140
reflected that all three of those questions
are critical to making sure we end up in the

01:07:19.140 --> 01:07:24.040
right place.
So, if I turn to some of the detail around

01:07:24.040 --> 01:07:28.940
the work that the individual workstreams have
been doing, the first on the operating model.

01:07:28.940 --> 01:07:34.000
What you see in this paper is starting to
try and tell the story of this operating model

01:07:34.000 --> 01:07:40.140
as opposed to going through the detail of
structures. And in particular, in the – in

01:07:40.140 --> 01:07:45.600
figure one, and in the description of that
over the paragraph eight onwards, it’s talking

01:07:45.600 --> 01:07:50.480
about the relative responsibilities, focus
and capabilities of the different parts of

01:07:50.480 --> 01:07:55.570
the model. One, around the national strategy,
support and services, which are provided at

01:07:55.570 --> 01:08:00.660
a corporate level. One around the regions
and what they’re responsible for in terms

01:08:00.660 --> 01:08:06.800
of the – them being the major locus of decision-making
and operational work with the rest of the

01:08:06.800 --> 01:08:12.099
NHS, and, indeed, with the STPs, ICSs and
devolved systems that are represented in the

01:08:12.099 --> 01:08:17.319
outside of the ring of that paper. So, all
of those parts have to be working together

01:08:17.319 --> 01:08:23.870
in order for this operating model to work.
And what that’s going to take is quite – is

01:08:23.870 --> 01:08:29.290
what we’ve described as – along with many
other institutions, as matrix-working, drawing

01:08:29.290 --> 01:08:36.339
on the expertise of nationally expert functions
and the operational relationships and day-to-day

01:08:36.339 --> 01:08:41.109
working that will be held in the regions.
And in paragraph 12, for example, we talk

01:08:41.109 --> 01:08:45.770
about some of the conditions that are going
to have to be in place and some of the changes

01:08:45.770 --> 01:08:51.450
in our core operating processes, in particular,
having a single internal planning process

01:08:51.450 --> 01:08:56.440
where the 20 people at the centre of this
organisation agree what our use of resources

01:08:56.440 --> 01:09:01.859
will be for the coming year or more than a
year. Supported by a flexible resourcing process

01:09:01.859 --> 01:09:05.130
that means we can get the right people in
the right place in the right time to support

01:09:05.130 --> 01:09:11.380
the rest of the NHS.
What that – what needs to happen then in

01:09:11.380 --> 01:09:15.839
order to support that operating model is,
we need to recognise that this is a shift

01:09:15.839 --> 01:09:22.020
in the capabilities and in the behaviours
of the people in this organisation, and we

01:09:22.020 --> 01:09:26.750
know that it’s incredibly difficult for
people to shift their behaviours, and that

01:09:26.750 --> 01:09:31.670
nothing changes unless the basic beliefs about
what we’re here for shift, back to my point

01:09:31.670 --> 01:09:35.880
about being clear what we’re here for in
the first place. So, we’re working in the

01:09:35.880 --> 01:09:40.710
culture and engagement workstream to think
about, how do we change the context of this

01:09:40.710 --> 01:09:44.730
organisation? How do we change the signals
that we provide people every day about what’s

01:09:44.730 --> 01:09:48.400
really important and what we’re here for,
so that we can work in a new, more joined-up

01:09:48.400 --> 01:09:53.540
way and build the right relationships so that
we can support the NHS in delivering the right

01:09:53.540 --> 01:09:57.150
care for patients?
And what that involves is changing the systems

01:09:57.150 --> 01:10:00.730
and processes of the organisation. I’ve
just mentioned a few. Another one is the joint

01:10:00.730 --> 01:10:05.960
governance paper that Malcolm referenced earlier.
It involves building the right capabilities

01:10:05.960 --> 01:10:10.130
in the organisation. It involves being clear
about what we’re here for and making that

01:10:10.130 --> 01:10:14.630
meaningful for everybody in the organisation.
And it involves being very clear about what

01:10:14.630 --> 01:10:20.400
behaviours and priorities we’re expecting
in our people.

01:10:20.400 --> 01:10:25.020
And we think that the answer to all of that
is already in the organisation. So, over the

01:10:25.020 --> 01:10:29.440
last few months, we’ve held a number of
workshops which have been open to anybody

01:10:29.440 --> 01:10:35.110
in the organisation to come and tell us, what
would you like the culture of this new joint

01:10:35.110 --> 01:10:39.770
entity to be, both from a perspective of what
would you like to change about what you experience

01:10:39.770 --> 01:10:43.780
now, but also, you know, with a blank sheet
of paper: what does it need to be like to

01:10:43.780 --> 01:10:48.060
deliver on the aspirations that we’ve set
out here? And some of the content is summarised

01:10:48.060 --> 01:10:54.670
at a very high level in the paper, but what’s
been I think very encouraging for all of us

01:10:54.670 --> 01:10:59.250
is that what we’ve heard from colleagues
in those workshops is very aligned between

01:10:59.250 --> 01:11:03.860
the two organisations. A lot of people have
said to us over the last few months, you know,

01:11:03.860 --> 01:11:07.070
‘These are two very, very different organisations;
it’s going to be incredibly challenging

01:11:07.070 --> 01:11:10.950
to bring them together’, and there are absolutely
differences that we have to pay attention

01:11:10.950 --> 01:11:17.200
to. But actually, the very strong intent of
the colleagues in both organisations is incredibly

01:11:17.200 --> 01:11:22.820
aligned, and hopefully, you see that content
reflected in the paper. And we’ve used not

01:11:22.820 --> 01:11:26.730
only our colleagues’ input here, but also
the broader system, so we’ve held a workshop

01:11:26.730 --> 01:11:32.580
with STP and ICS leaders, we held a session
at the Expo conference at the beginning of

01:11:32.580 --> 01:11:36.870
September where we invited anybody who was
at Expo – so that included patient representatives,

01:11:36.870 --> 01:11:42.310
it included frontline clinicians – and overall,
that’s been about 500 people who’ve had

01:11:42.310 --> 01:11:47.770
a chance to give us some input into what it’s
going to take to make it happen. And we’ve

01:11:47.770 --> 01:11:54.310
included on page seven an outline plan, but
which has a bunch of detail behind it, that

01:11:54.310 --> 01:11:58.620
says, we’re – over the next few months,
we’re going to run this process of actually

01:11:58.620 --> 01:12:04.210
really, with the great intent, shifting the
culture of the organisation to support improved

01:12:04.210 --> 01:12:08.130
ways of working, so that by the time we also
– we have the structural elements in place,

01:12:08.130 --> 01:12:11.241
we also hopefully have at least the beginnings
of the behaviours that it’s going to take

01:12:11.241 --> 01:12:15.820
to make it happen.
I was going to summarise the governance paper,

01:12:15.820 --> 01:12:18.280
but I think, Malcolm, given what you said,
I’ll pause there.

01:12:18.280 --> 01:12:27.500
Good. Thank you very much, Emily. Let me invite
any questions or comments. Understanding that

01:12:27.500 --> 01:12:32.960
this is work in progress, quite a way to go,
but I would pay tribute, I think, to the amount

01:12:32.960 --> 01:12:37.500
of progress that’s been made already. We
do start from very different starting points

01:12:37.500 --> 01:12:43.160
between the two organisations, and what is
very clear from the paper is that there’s

01:12:43.160 --> 01:12:46.690
a great deal of common ground in terms of
vision and aspiration: a golden thread, I

01:12:46.690 --> 01:12:52.400
think I saw a comment on. But actually, still
bringing our combined forces together along

01:12:52.400 --> 01:12:57.640
a single track is going to be quite complicated.
Kathy, did you want to come in?

01:12:57.640 --> 01:13:03.840
Yes. So, I mean, thank you, Emily, for the
paper, and I just wanted to comment that I

01:13:03.840 --> 01:13:08.530
think it’s really encouraging to hear that
there is common ground between – you know,

01:13:08.530 --> 01:13:14.219
more commonality, my sense is, and the fact
that so many people have got themselves involved.

01:13:14.219 --> 01:13:19.520
I think it’s obviously progressed a lot
over the last few weeks since we last kind

01:13:19.520 --> 01:13:26.560
of discussed it. But notwithstanding that,
there’s clearly a huge effort ahead in order

01:13:26.560 --> 01:13:30.370
to sort of bring people – you know, changing
– we always say how hard it is to change

01:13:30.370 --> 01:13:36.060
cultures, and with a clear intent about where
we want to go, I think you’re probably right,

01:13:36.060 --> 01:13:38.989
aren’t you, that the people themselves have
got the answers.

01:13:38.989 --> 01:13:42.270
Yes.
But we absolutely need to make sure we model

01:13:42.270 --> 01:13:47.410
the environment in which that can flourish,
otherwise, it will frankly fall at the first

01:13:47.410 --> 01:13:51.900
hurdle; they’ll have a lot of expectations
which will be dashed because they don’t

01:13:51.900 --> 01:13:57.480
see that, and I think that it’s almost incumbent
on us right now to pledge that we will, in

01:13:57.480 --> 01:14:01.520
everything we do, from, you know, here on
forth, if we haven’t already been doing

01:14:01.520 --> 01:14:05.920
it, then ensure that we are modelling the
behaviours we want to see, because it’s

01:14:05.920 --> 01:14:07.660
very easy to dash these things –
Yeah.

01:14:07.660 --> 01:14:13.020
– you know, within all that sort of sense
of enthusiasm. So, I just wanted to say thank

01:14:13.020 --> 01:14:20.320
you, but there’s a lot to do.
Thanks, Kathy. Well, I think the starting

01:14:20.320 --> 01:14:24.630
point is the existential question of ‘What
are we here for?’, which many of us wonder

01:14:24.630 --> 01:14:30.360
about every day, but – on a personal level.
But I think on this –

01:14:30.360 --> 01:14:33.309
Have you got another poem coming?
No, no, I think on this, actually, it’s

01:14:33.309 --> 01:14:37.969
not what we were here for; it’s for something
greater. It’s for something that joins up

01:14:37.969 --> 01:14:41.500
across the divide and across the siloes that
we’ve been having to work across in the

01:14:41.500 --> 01:14:48.060
past inefficiently and wastefully. So, I do
commend this work. I think it’s fantastic.

01:14:48.060 --> 01:14:51.250
On – in terms of the governance paper, I
don’t think I need to take that any further;

01:14:51.250 --> 01:14:56.050
I think everybody’s up to speed with that.
So, I think that brings us to the end of that

01:14:56.050 --> 01:14:57.719
item.
And if I’m right, it brings us to the end

